{:edb/db-before-t 1882 :edb/db-after-t 1883 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535195 50 #inst "2026-10-08T19:18:13.200Z" 13194139535195 true] [17592186179945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186177670 13194139535195 false] [17592186179945 1017 #edb/ref 17592186336846 13194139535195 true] [17592186179945 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" 13194139535195 true] [17592186179945 1100 "Which of the following is equivalent to $∫_(C)2ydx + \\ln xdy + 3zdz$, where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t) = i + tj + t^{2}k$ for $t ∈ [1, 3]$?" 13194139535195 false] [17592186179945 1145 "Along $C$, $x(t)=1$, $y(t)=t$, and $z(t)=t^2$, so $x'(t)=0$, $y'(t)=1$, and $z'(t)=2t$. Substitute both the coordinates and their derivatives:\n\n$$\\int_C 2y\\,dx+\\ln x\\,dy+3z\\,dz\n=\\int_1^3\\left(2t\\cdot0+\\ln(1)\\cdot1+3t^2\\cdot2t\\right)\\,dt\n=\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the equivalent integral is $\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt$, and its value is $[\\tfrac32t^4]_1^3=120$.\n\nThe original source accepted $\\int_1^3 6t^2\\,dt$ and its worked solution changed $3t^2\\cdot2t$ into $6t^2$. That simplification loses a factor of $t$. The study choice replaces that exponent with $3$; all other original choices are retained." 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186177671 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186177673 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186177674 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186177675 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1000 #edb/ref 17592186232883 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1041 "selection" 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1104 #edb/ref 17592186232883 13194139535195 true] [17592186336846 1110 #uuid "c4b5b1f8-2205-55dd-8481-133a50f9b030" 13194139535195 true]] :edb/tempids {"q-124871/mathematical-cor